{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"\n\nF1 Verdikt: **A + S2** — Begründung: Der Empfänger benötigt einen eigenen Akt im eigenen Ledger, um ACKED explizit von CLOSED zu unterscheiden — das löst den Kern des unterscheidungslosen Zustands. Option A stellt die seit Commit eb2f1b749 zurückgenommene Absicht (−202 Zeilen `xfer ack`-Arm) wieder her und gibt dem Empfänger die Selbst-Erledigung zurück, ohne den Emitter-Ledger zu modifizieren. S2 (weiter listen mit Zustandsspalte) ist preferable gegenüber S1, weil `--addressed` sonst geschlossen-verdiente Blöcke aktiv ausblendet und damit Informationsverlust einführt, statt nur den Blick zu filtern. — Riss: Der frühere Rückbau von `xfer ack` (eb2f1b749) deutet auf nicht-dokumentierte Probleme hin (z. B. Deadlock-Risiko bei gegenseitigem ACK, oder Komplexität bei Re-Reconciliation); ohne Dokumentation des ursprünglichen Abwägung ist A ein Wiederaufbau ins Unbekannte.\n\nF2 Verdikt: **A (per-SHA + `cancel-in-progress: true`)** — Begründung: Das Gate prüft den HEAD-Zustand des Registers, nicht je-SHA-Artefakte — also ist der relevante Adressraum der SHA selbst. Per-SHA-Gruppierung mit `cancel-in-progress: true` löscht stehende Vorgänger (wie gemessen: sieben `cancelled` von acht Läufen in 10 Min), während der laufende Probe-SHA durchläuft. Per-ref + true (B) würde bei Pushes auf main zwar auch cancelln, vermischt aber verschiedene HEAD-SHAs in derselben Gruppe, was die GLM-/DeepSeek-/Lumo-Argumentation (\"der HEAD ist je SHA definiert\") ignoriert. Debounce/Batch (C) wäre zusätzlich, ersetzt aber nicht die Gruppierungsfrage. — Riss: Kimis Einwand bleibt bestehen — ein Cancel mid-write kann inkonsistenten Registerzustand hinterlassen; `cancelled`-Läufe müssen vom Gate zuverlässig ignoriert werden, sonst prüft man auf halbfertige Writes.\n\nF3 Verdikt: **B** — Begründung: Die Leitstelle verteilt Verdikte als `## An <linie>`-Blöcke automatisch in das lebende Handover der jeweiligen Empfängerlinie, ohne die Architektur des Rust-Lesers zu berühren — das folgt der bestehenden Formatkonvention und vermeidet den Architekturpfad-Constraint (R-2026-10-18-d/e: Linien dürfen Architektur nicht selbst ändern, brauchen RAT-ID). Option A würde `state/stimmen/` als neue Dokumentwurzel in den Leser einkodieren und damit genau den Architekturpfad triggern, den die Regel verbietet. Option C verschiebt das Problem nur (wer pflegt den Index?), D perpetuiert den manuellen Fehler von heute. — Riss: Bei starkem Verdiktvolumen entsteht Duplikation über die Handover-Dateien aller betroffenen Linien; das Handover wird zum Kopierziel für jeden neuen Eintrag. — Zusatz: **ja**, weil eine RAT-ID bereits vor dem Bau der Wahl im Index stehen muss — sonst läuft die Änderung ohne Ratssitz, was gegen R-2026-10-18-d/e verstößt.","reasoning":"The user wants me to act as a \"Ratssitz\" (council seat) in the omegaflow house, answering three architectural questions. Let me analyze each question carefully based on the provided context and evidence.\n\n**Question 1: Where is the receipt recorded in the Token-Ledger?**\n\nThe problem: Today, a token's state only lives in the sender's (emitter's) ledger. The recipient cannot acknowledge their own receipt - `xfer close` on someone else's token is a hard error. ACK and CLOSED both read as CLOSED - indistinguishable. Also, `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` stays at 0, completed reads as open. An early `xfer ack` arm was rolled back (commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines).\n\n\nLooking at the options, A seems most aligned with the original intent—it adds an explicit acknowledgment action in the recipient's ledger that distinguishes ACKED from CLOSED, which directly addresses the current indistinguishability problem. B maintains the status quo, C derives state read-only which feels incomplete, and D would require a ledger per token instead of per line. The secondary question is whether `--addressed` should hide closed blocks (S1) or continue listing them with a status column (S2).\n\nThe strongest argument against A is the prior rollback of the xfer ack feature—the fact that 202 lines were removed suggests there were real issues with that approach that need to be understood before revisiting it.\n\nFor the second question about concurrency keys, the data shows that 5-7 gates fire per push to main, with 25 different HEAD-SHAs in the last 100 runs and 7 cancelled runs out of 8 within 10 minutes. The core tension is between per-SHA semantics, where each SHA has its own defined register state, versus per-ref semantics, where the reference itself is what matters. the same SHA. With `cancel-in-progress: true`, new pushes to main would cancel stale `register-coverage` runs from earlier SHAs, but the per-SHA group would still maintain isolation for any concurrent runs on different branches. The key insight is that this balances the cancellation behavior with proper SHA-level deduplication. The strongest concern is Kimi's point about cancellations mid-write potentially leaving the register in an inconsistent state, but since the gate ignores `cancelled` jobs and only validates the HEAD's current state, using per-SHA grouping with `cancel-in-progress: true` makes sense — stale runs get cancelled while the latest SHA's run completes and validates correctly.\n\nNow I'm looking at a separate issue: `register_lookup` doesn't search `state/stimmen/`, so it's returning zero hits when grepping for Stimmen. This means the Rat's verdicts aren't being surfaced through the register, and lines would need to manually receive them from the Leitstelle to know what's pending for them.\n\nFor the fix, I'm weighing two main approaches: either add `state/stimmen/` as a search root in the Rust reader (which is simpler to implement but creates architectural coupling), or derive the verdicts into each line's living handover by extracting the `## An <linie>` blocks (which keeps things separated but requires more upfront work).\n\nThere's also a constraint from earlier questions that lines can't modify architecture themselves—they'd need a RAT-ID for that—so the question becomes whether choosing option A or B requires architectural approval or if it's something the team can decide independently.\n\nGiven that constraint, option A (adding the directory as a search root) seems like the pragmatic middle ground—it's a small code change but technically touches the architecture, so it probably needs RAT-ID approval first. Option B would sidestep that issue entirely since it works with existing mechanisms. Though the most minimal solution might be option C, which just creates a dedicated index file that the lookup can read without modifying the core reader logic.\n","reasoning_content":"The user wants me to act as a \"Ratssitz\" (council seat) in the omegaflow house, answering three architectural questions. Let me analyze each question carefully based on the provided context and evidence.\n\n**Question 1: Where is the receipt recorded in the Token-Ledger?**\n\nThe problem: Today, a token's state only lives in the sender's (emitter's) ledger. The recipient cannot acknowledge their own receipt - `xfer close` on someone else's token is a hard error. ACK and CLOSED both read as CLOSED - indistinguishable. Also, `register_lookup --addressed <linie>` lists closed blocks further; `--fired` stays at 0, completed reads as open. An early `xfer ack` arm was rolled back (commit eb2f1b749, -202 lines).\n\n\nLooking at the options, A seems most aligned with the original intent—it adds an explicit acknowledgment action in the recipient's ledger that distinguishes ACKED from CLOSED, which directly addresses the current indistinguishability problem. B maintains the status quo, C derives state read-only which feels incomplete, and D would require a ledger per token instead of per line. The secondary question is whether `--addressed` should hide closed blocks (S1) or continue listing them with a status column (S2).\n\nThe strongest argument against A is the prior rollback of the xfer ack feature—the fact that 202 lines were removed suggests there were real issues with that approach that need to be understood before revisiting it.\n\nFor the second question about concurrency keys, the data shows that 5-7 gates fire per push to main, with 25 different HEAD-SHAs in the last 100 runs and 7 cancelled runs out of 8 within 10 minutes. The core tension is between per-SHA semantics, where each SHA has its own defined register state, versus per-ref semantics, where the reference itself is what matters. the same SHA. With `cancel-in-progress: true`, new pushes to main would cancel stale `register-coverage` runs from earlier SHAs, but the per-SHA group would still maintain isolation for any concurrent runs on different branches. The key insight is that this balances the cancellation behavior with proper SHA-level deduplication. The strongest concern is Kimi's point about cancellations mid-write potentially leaving the register in an inconsistent state, but since the gate ignores `cancelled` jobs and only validates the HEAD's current state, using per-SHA grouping with `cancel-in-progress: true` makes sense — stale runs get cancelled while the latest SHA's run completes and validates correctly.\n\nNow I'm looking at a separate issue: `register_lookup` doesn't search `state/stimmen/`, so it's returning zero hits when grepping for Stimmen. This means the Rat's verdicts aren't being surfaced through the register, and lines would need to manually receive them from the Leitstelle to know what's pending for them.\n\nFor the fix, I'm weighing two main approaches: either add `state/stimmen/` as a search root in the Rust reader (which is simpler to implement but creates architectural coupling), or derive the verdicts into each line's living handover by extracting the `## An <linie>` blocks (which keeps things separated but requires more upfront work).\n\nThere's also a constraint from earlier questions that lines can't modify architecture themselves—they'd need a RAT-ID for that—so the question becomes whether choosing option A or B requires architectural approval or if it's something the team can decide independently.\n\nGiven that constraint, option A (adding the directory as a search root) seems like the pragmatic middle ground—it's a small code change but technically touches the architecture, so it probably needs RAT-ID approval first. Option B would sidestep that issue entirely since it works with existing mechanisms. Though the most minimal solution might be option C, which just creates a dedicated index file that the lookup can read without modifying the core reader logic.\n","role":"assistant"}}],"created":1792312735,"id":"db67c363c49f8464","model":"agnes-2-5-flash:free","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":1573,"prompt_tokens":1474,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":256},"total_tokens":3047}}